    deepExplanation: "Step 1 — Understand the topic.\n\n**REST API Testing** should verify request shape, response mapping, errors, auth, retries, cancellation, caching, and contract compatibility.\n\nStep 2 — Easy method:\nDefine the observable behavior, inputs, expected result, failure conditions, and cleanup. Use the smallest test level that provides enough confidence.\n\nStep 3 — WITHOUT BUILT-IN / CORE TypeScript:\nThe manual version exposes the core logic so it is easy to explain line by line in an interview.\n\n```ts\ninterface ApiResult<T> { status: number; data?: T; }\n\nfunction isSuccess<T>(result: ApiResult<T>): boolean {\n  return result.status >= 200 && result.status < 300;\n}\n\nconst success = isSuccess({\n  status: 200,\n  data: { id: \"1\" },\n});\n```\n\nStep 4 — WITH BUILT-IN / practical TypeScript:\nUse native array/collection helpers when they make the same behavior clearer without hiding the important test idea.\n\n```ts\nconst response = { status: 200, data: { id: \"1\" } };\n\nconst success =\n  response.status >= 200 &&\n  response.status < 300;\n```\n\nStep 5 — Production example:\nTest request/response contracts, auth, errors, retries, cancellation, and caching at the API boundary.\n\nStep 6 — Test quality:\nKeep the test deterministic, isolated, diagnostic, and representative. Cover success, boundary, failure, accessibility, and cleanup where relevant.\n\nStep 7 — Senior interview takeaway:\nExplain why this belongs at this test level, what it deliberately does not cover, how flakiness is prevented, and how it fits the wider suite.",
